.TH "CANOE" "1" "November 2026" ""
.SH NAME
canoe \- a stacking Wayland compositor with Win 3.x looks
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]canoe\f[R] [\f[B]\-n\f[R]] [\f[B]\-s\f[R] \f[I]COMMAND\f[R]]\&...
[\f[B]\-\-backend\f[R] \f[I]BACKEND\f[R]] [\f[B]\-\-no\-xwayland\f[R]]
.PP
\f[B]canoe\f[R] \f[B]\-\-share\-picker\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]canoe\f[R] \f[B]\-h\f[R] | \f[B]\-V\f[R]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Canoe is a stacking Wayland compositor and window manager.
Windows overlap and are moved and resized freely; Canoe draws classic
window borders and titlebars around every program that leaves its
decorations to the window manager, rather than drawing its own or asking
to.
Minimized windows become icons on the desktop, and right\-clicking the
empty desktop opens a menu of all windows.
.PP
Started from a text console (VT), with seatd or systemd\-logind managing
the seat, Canoe takes over the monitors and input devices of that seat.
Started inside a running Wayland or X11 session, it opens a window
instead, which is handy for trying it out.
.PP
X11 programs run through Xwayland, which Canoe starts if it is
installed.
Programs Canoe launches find it through \f[B]DISPLAY\f[R].
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\f[B]\-n\f[R], \f[B]\-\-no\-config\f[R]
Ignore \f[I]\(ti/.config/canoe/canoe.toml\f[R] and use the built\-in
defaults.
Reloads keep honoring this.
.TP
\f[B]\-s\f[R] \f[I]COMMAND\f[R], \f[B]\-\-startup\f[R]=\f[I]COMMAND\f[R]
Run \f[I]COMMAND\f[R] in the session once it is up, after the
configuration\(cqs \f[B]startup_cmds\f[R].
Each is run through \f[B]sh \-c\f[R], so arguments, quoting and
\f[I]$VARIABLES\f[R] work as in a shell.
May be given more than once; the commands start in the order given.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-backend\f[R]=\f[I]BACKEND\f[R]
Where to show the session:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]udev\f[R] (alias \f[B]drm\f[R])
On the monitors of this seat.
The default on a text console.
.TP
\f[B]winit\f[R] (alias \f[B]nested\f[R])
In a window of the running session.
The default when \f[B]WAYLAND_DISPLAY\f[R] or \f[B]DISPLAY\f[R] is set.
Only available if Canoe was built with the \f[B]winit\f[R] feature,
which is on by default.
.TP
\f[B]headless\f[R]
Without any monitor, for automated tests.
The outputs are taken from \f[B]CANOE_HEADLESS_OUTPUTS\f[R] (see
ENVIRONMENT).
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-\-no\-xwayland\f[R]
Don\(cqt start Xwayland, so X11 programs don\(cqt run.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-share\-picker\f[R]
Act as the screen\-sharing chooser of
\f[B]xdg\-desktop\-portal\-wlr\f[R]: ask the running Canoe what to
share, print the answer and exit.
See SCREEN SHARING.
.TP
\f[B]\-h\f[R], \f[B]\-\-help\f[R]
Print a short help and exit.
.TP
\f[B]\-V\f[R], \f[B]\-\-version\f[R]
Print the version and exit.
.SH KEYBOARD SHORTCUTS
\f[I]Super\f[R] stands for the main modifier, which can be changed to
\f[I]Alt\f[R] with \f[B]main_modifier\f[R] in the configuration file.
A hotkey for the same chord (see CONFIGURATION) overrides a shortcut.
.TP
\f[B]Super+Shift+Return\f[R]
Open a terminal (\f[B]terminal_cmd\f[R], default \f[B]foot\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]Super+Space\f[R]
Open the application launcher (\f[B]launcher_cmd\f[R], default
\f[B]fuzzel\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]Super+L\f[R]
Lock the screen (\f[B]lock_cmd\f[R], default \f[B]swaylock\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]Super+W\f[R]
Close the focused window.
.TP
\f[B]Super+Tab\f[R], \f[B]Super+Shift+Tab\f[R]
Focus the next or previous window.
.TP
\f[B]Super+\(ga\f[R]
Focus the next window of the same application.
.TP
\f[B]Super+Return\f[R]
Toggle fullscreen.
.TP
\f[B]Super+Up\f[R]
Maximize the focused window.
.TP
\f[B]Super+Down\f[R]
Unfullscreen or unmaximize the focused window; otherwise minimize it.
.TP
\f[B]Super+H\f[R], \f[B]Super+M\f[R]
Minimize the focused window.
.TP
\f[B]Super+D\f[R]
Minimize all windows; press again to bring them back.
.TP
\f[B]Super+Left\f[R], \f[B]Super+Right\f[R]
Snap the focused window to the left or right half of the output; restore
it if it is snapped to the other side.
.TP
\f[B]Super+Shift+\f[R]\f[I]Arrow\f[R]
Focus the output in that direction.
The window last used there gets the focus, and the pointer jumps to the
middle of the output.
.TP
\f[B]Super+Alt+\f[R]\f[I]Arrow\f[R]
Send the focused window, or the selected desktop icon, to the output in
that direction.
.PP
Minimizing the focused window with \f[B]Super+Down\f[R],
\f[B]Super+H\f[R] or \f[B]Super+M\f[R] selects its desktop icon for as
long as \f[I]Super\f[R] stays pressed, so \f[B]Super+Up\f[R] brings the
window right back and \f[B]Super+Alt+\f[R]\f[I]Arrow\f[R] sends the icon
to another output.
Releasing \f[I]Super\f[R] focuses the next window.
.PP
A clicked desktop icon stays selected and has the keyboard:
.TP
\f[I]Arrow\f[R], \f[B]Tab\f[R], \f[B]Shift+Tab\f[R]
Select the icon next to it.
.TP
\f[B]Super+Up\f[R], \f[B]Return\f[R]
Bring the selected icon\(cqs window back.
.TP
\f[B]Escape\f[R]
Deselect the icon and focus the last window again.
.SH MOUSE ACTIONS
.TP
Click on a window
Focus it.
.TP
Drag the titlebar
Move the window.
.TP
Double\-click the titlebar
Maximize or restore the window.
.TP
Drag a window edge
Resize the window.
.TP
\f[B]Super\f[R]+left drag
Move the window, wherever it is grabbed.
.TP
\f[B]Super\f[R]+right drag
Resize the window, wherever it is grabbed.
.TP
Right\-click the empty desktop
Open the window menu.
.TP
Left\-click the empty desktop
Unfocus the current window and deselect icons.
.TP
Double\-click a desktop icon
Bring its window back.
.SH MULTIPLE OUTPUTS
Windows belong to the output they were opened or last moved on, and keep
their place on it when the output layout changes.
When an output is unplugged, its windows move to the output in use, or
to the nearest remaining one, keeping their relative position.
.PP
Canoe remembers how the outputs are set up for each combination of
connected monitors, and sets them up that way again when that
combination comes back, e.g.\ when docking a laptop.
Set them up once with any tool that speaks wlr\-output\-management, such
as \f[B]wdisplays\f[R](1) or \f[B]wlr\-randr\f[R](1).
Monitors are recognized by make, model and serial number.
On a laptop, whether the lid is closed counts as part of the
combination.
.PP
Each combination can have a primary output: the one new windows open on
when monitors come or go, and the one launchers and panels without an
output of their own appear on.
Output tools choose it through Canoe\(cqs experimental
\f[B]xx\-primary\-output\-v1\f[R] protocol.
.SH DESKTOP ICONS
A minimized window\(cqs icon is the first of these that exists:
.IP "1." 3
\f[I]\(ti/.config/canoe/icons/APP\-ID.svg\f[R] or \f[I].png\f[R];
.IP "2." 3
the icon the program set for the window (xdg\-toplevel\-icon);
.IP "3." 3
the \f[B]Icon=\f[R] of the program\(cqs desktop entry,
\f[I]APP\-ID.desktop\f[R];
.IP "4." 3
the first letter of the window\(cqs title.
.PP
Programs can draw a minimized window\(cqs icon themselves with
Canoe\(cqs experimental \f[B]xx\-iconlet\-v1\f[R] protocol.
An X11 window\(cqs app id is the class of its \f[B]WM_CLASS\f[R].
.SH CONFIGURATION
Canoe reads \f[I]\(ti/.config/canoe/canoe.toml\f[R].
Every setting is optional.
If the file can\(cqt be parsed, Canoe ignores all of it and uses the
built\-in defaults.
Send \f[B]SIGHUP\f[R] to re\-read it without restarting.
A sample listing every setting with its default comes with Canoe as
\f[I]contrib/canoe.toml\f[R].
.SS Top\-level settings
.TP
\f[B]main_modifier\f[R] = \(lqsuper\(rq | \(lqalt\(rq
Modifier for the built\-in shortcuts.
Default: \(lqsuper\(rq.
.TP
\f[B]window_list_scope\f[R] = \(lqoutput\(rq | \(lqall\(rq
Whether \f[B]Super+Tab\f[R], \f[B]Super+\(ga\f[R] and the window menu
list the windows on the output they open on, or on every output.
Default: \(lqoutput\(rq.
.TP
\f[B]launcher_cmd\f[R], \f[B]terminal_cmd\f[R], \f[B]lock_cmd\f[R]
Programs for the launcher, terminal and screen locker shortcuts.
Each is a program name or an argv array,
e.g.\ \f[CR][\(dqswaylock\(dq, \(dq\-f\(dq, \(dq\-c\(dq, \(dq000000\(dq]\f[R].
A plain string is not run through a shell.
Defaults: \(lqfuzzel\(rq, \(lqfoot\(rq, \(lqswaylock\(rq.
.TP
\f[B]startup_cmds\f[R]
Programs launched once when Canoe has set up the desktop, in order,
without waiting for one to finish.
Each entry is a program name or an argv array.
They don\(cqt run again on reload.
.TP
\f[B]xcursor_theme\f[R], \f[B]xcursor_size\f[R]
XCursor theme and size (in logical pixels) for the cursors Canoe draws.
Also set as \f[B]XCURSOR_THEME\f[R] and \f[B]XCURSOR_SIZE\f[R] for
programs Canoe launches.
Unset by default: Canoe then looks at the \f[B][env]\f[R] table and its
own environment, falling back to the \(lqdefault\(rq theme at size 24.
A theme that is not installed is replaced by one that is.
.TP
\f[B]xwayland_hidpi\f[R] = true | false
Let X11 programs draw at the outputs\(cq scale, rounded up to a whole
number, so they are crisp.
With false they draw at scale 1 and are stretched.
Default: true.
.TP
\f[B]update_activation_environment\f[R] = true | false
On startup, export \f[B]WAYLAND_DISPLAY\f[R], \f[B]DISPLAY\f[R],
\f[B]XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP\f[R], \f[B]XDG_SESSION_TYPE\f[R] and, if set,
\f[B]XCURSOR_THEME\f[R] and \f[B]XCURSOR_SIZE\f[R] to the systemd user
manager and D\-Bus activation environment, so portals started on demand
find this session.
Default: true.
The headless backend never exports anything.
.TP
\f[B]remember_outputs\f[R] = true | false
Remember and restore output layouts and the primary output (see MULTIPLE
OUTPUTS).
With false, new outputs are lined up left to right.
Default: true.
.TP
\f[B]emulate_sway\f[R] = true | false
Answer sway\(cqs IPC on the socket \f[B]SWAYSOCK\f[R] names (set for
programs Canoe launches), telling where outputs and windows are and
which is focused, for screenshot tools like \f[B]shotman\f[R] and
\f[B]grimshot\f[R].
Commands and events are not supported.
Default: false.
.TP
\f[B]emulate_kwin\f[R] = true | false
Take the names \f[B]org.kde.KWin\f[R] and
\f[B]org.kde.KWin.ScreenShot2\f[R] on the session bus and offer
KWin\(cqs screenshot interface, for \f[B]spectacle\f[R](1).
Default: false.
.SS [env]
Environment variables set for every program Canoe launches: startup
commands, the launcher, terminal, screen locker and hotkey commands.
They do not change Canoe\(cqs own environment.
Values must be strings.
.IP
.EX
[env]
QT_QPA_PLATFORM = \(dqwayland;xcb\(dq
ELECTRON_OZONE_PLATFORM_HINT = \(dqauto\(dq
.EE
.SS [keyboard]
.TP
\f[B]layout\f[R], \f[B]variant\f[R], \f[B]options\f[R], \f[B]rules\f[R], \f[B]model\f[R]
The XKB keymap, with comma\-separated lists as in
\f[I]/usr/share/X11/xkb/rules/base.lst\f[R].
Whatever is left out comes from \f[B]XKB_DEFAULT_LAYOUT\f[R] and friends
in Canoe\(cqs own environment, then from libxkbcommon\(cqs defaults
(\(lqus\(rq).
\f[B]options = \(lq\(lq\f[R] turns off any from
\f[B]XKB_DEFAULT_OPTIONS\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]repeat_rate\f[R], \f[B]repeat_delay\f[R]
Keys per second, and milliseconds before a held key repeats.
.PP
With more than one layout, switch between them with an XKB option such
as \f[B]grp:alt_shift_toggle\f[R], or with a hotkey.
Shortcuts and hotkeys name Latin keys and keep working while a layout
without them is active.
.SS [hotkeys]
Key chords that run commands.
Each key is a \f[B]+\f[R]\-separated chord, modifiers first; each value
is a command, either a string split on whitespace or an argv array taken
verbatim.
Instead of a command, \f[B]{ switch_layout = \(lqnext\(rq }\f[R],
\f[B]\(lqprevious\(rq\f[R] or a layout\(cqs place in the list (counting
from 1) switches the keyboard layout.
.PP
Modifiers are \f[B]super\f[R] (\f[B]logo\f[R], \f[B]win\f[R],
\f[B]mod4\f[R]), \f[B]alt\f[R] (\f[B]mod1\f[R]), \f[B]ctrl\f[R]
(\f[B]control\f[R]), \f[B]shift\f[R], \f[B]mod3\f[R] and \f[B]mod5\f[R].
Keys are XKB keysym names (\f[B]i\f[R], \f[B]Return\f[R],
\f[B]space\f[R], \f[B]F5\f[R], \f[B]XF86AudioRaiseVolume\f[R], \&...),
matched case\-insensitively; add \f[B]Shift\f[R] explicitly to mean the
shifted chord.
.IP
.EX
[hotkeys]
\(dqSuper+I\(dq = \(dqcontrol\-panel\(dq
\(dqSuper+E\(dq = [\(dqsh\(dq, \(dq\-c\(dq, \(dqnotify\-send \(aqhi there\(aq && foot\(dq]
\(dqCtrl+Alt+space\(dq = { switch_layout = \(dqnext\(dq }
.EE
.SS [ui]
Look of window frames, menus and the desktop.
Colors are \f[B]#RGB\f[R], \f[B]#RRGGBB\f[R], \f[B]#RRGGBBAA\f[R] or
\f[B]\(lqtransparent\(rq\f[R].
Sizes are in logical pixels and scale with the output.
.TP
\f[B]border_width\f[R]
Width of window borders.
Default: 5.
.TP
\f[B]border_active\f[R], \f[B]border_inactive\f[R]
Border colors as \f[B]{ outer = \&..., mid = \&..., inner = \&...
}\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]titlebar_bg_active\f[R], \f[B]titlebar_bg_inactive\f[R], \f[B]titlebar_text_active\f[R], \f[B]titlebar_text_inactive\f[R]
Titlebar colors.
.TP
\f[B]titlebar_text_align\f[R] = \(lqcenter\(rq | \(lqleft\(rq
Where window titles go.
Default: \(lqcenter\(rq.
.TP
\f[B]menu_bg\f[R], \f[B]menu_text\f[R], \f[B]menu_highlight_bg\f[R], \f[B]menu_highlight_text\f[R]
Menu colors.
.TP
\f[B]button_bg\f[R], \f[B]button_highlight\f[R], \f[B]button_shadow\f[R]
Titlebar button colors.
.TP
\f[B]shadows_enabled\f[R], \f[B]shadows_active_size\f[R], \f[B]shadows_inactive_size\f[R], \f[B]shadows_color\f[R]
Soft drop shadows below windows and menus.
Defaults: true, 25, 12, \(lq#00000033\(rq.
.TP
\f[B]font_name\f[R], \f[B]font_size\f[R]
Font for titles and menus, as a fontconfig pattern.
Defaults: \(lqsans\(rq, 15.0.
.TP
\f[B]desktop_background\f[R]
Fill color of the desktop.
Default: \(lq#008080\(rq.
Use \(lqtransparent\(rq when a program like \f[B]swaybg\f[R](1) draws
the wallpaper.
.TP
\f[B]desktop_menu\f[R] = true | false
Whether the empty desktop takes clicks (to open the window menu and
unfocus windows).
With false, only icons do and every other click goes to the wallpaper
below.
Default: true.
.TP
\f[B]icons_enabled\f[R] = true | false
Show minimized windows as desktop icons.
Default: true.
.TP
\f[B]icons_font_name\f[R], \f[B]icons_font_size\f[R], \f[B]icons_text\f[R], \f[B]icons_highlight_bg\f[R], \f[B]icons_highlight_text\f[R]
Look of icon labels.
Default to values derived from the font and menu settings above.
.TP
\f[B]animations_enabled\f[R], \f[B]animations_duration\f[R]
Let windows glide into place when maximized, snapped or restored, and
shrink into their icons when minimized.
Duration in milliseconds; 0 turns animations off.
Defaults: true, 150.
.SS [[rules]]
Per\-window rules.
A window matches a rule if its app id matches any of
\f[B]match_app_id\f[R] (exact), \f[B]match_app_id_prefix\f[R] or
\f[B]app_id_regex\f[R], or its title matches \f[B]title\f[R] (exact) or
\f[B]title_regex\f[R], and it has all properties listed in
\f[B]match_props\f[R]:
.TP
\f[B]toplevel\f[R]
The window has no parent.
.TP
\f[B]csd_only\f[R]
The program draws its own decorations: it can\(cqt be asked not to (e.g.
libadwaita programs) or asks to (e.g.\ Chromium without the system
titlebar).
.PP
A matching rule can set:
.TP
\f[B]force_ssd\f[R] = true
Draw Canoe\(cqs decorations even for \f[B]csd_only\f[R] windows.
.TP
\f[B]swallow_top\f[R] = \f[I]PIXELS\f[R]
Hide that many pixels at the top of the window, e.g.\ to remove a
program\(cqs own titlebar.
.IP
.EX
[[rules]]
match_app_id = [\(dqfirefox\(dq, \(dqfirefox\-esr\(dq]
match_props = \(dqtoplevel\(dq
force_ssd = true
swallow_top = 48
.EE
.SH SCREEN SHARING
Programs share the screen through the screencast portal, which for Canoe
is \f[B]xdg\-desktop\-portal\-wlr\f[R] (0.8 or newer for single
windows).
Route the portals with \f[I]canoe\-portals.conf\f[R] (shipped as
\f[I]contrib/canoe\-portals.conf\f[R], installed into
\f[I]/usr/share/xdg\-desktop\-portal/\f[R] or
\f[I]\(ti/.config/xdg\-desktop\-portal/\f[R]) and make Canoe the chooser
in \f[I]\(ti/.config/xdg\-desktop\-portal\-wlr/config\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
[screencast]
chooser_type=dmenu
chooser_cmd=canoe \-\-share\-picker
.EE
.PP
When a program wants to share, Canoe dims all screens and highlights the
window or screen under the pointer.
Click to share it, or use \f[B]Tab\f[R], the arrow keys and
\f[B]Return\f[R]; \f[B]Escape\f[R] or a right click cancels.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
\f[B]SIGHUP\f[R]
Re\-read the configuration file: theme, desktop, window rules, keyboard,
hotkeys and screenshot emulation are updated.
Startup commands don\(cqt run again.
.TP
\f[B]SIGINT\f[R], \f[B]SIGTERM\f[R], \f[B]SIGQUIT\f[R]
Quit.
.TP
\f[B]SIGUSR1\f[R]
With the headless backend and \f[B]CANOE_HEADLESS_LID\f[R] set, open or
close the lid.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.SS Read by Canoe
.TP
\f[B]WAYLAND_DISPLAY\f[R], \f[B]DISPLAY\f[R]
If either is set, Canoe defaults to the \f[B]winit\f[R] backend.
For \f[B]\-\-share\-picker\f[R], \f[B]WAYLAND_DISPLAY\f[R] names the
Canoe to ask.
.TP
\f[B]XDG_RUNTIME_DIR\f[R]
Where the sockets for \f[B]\-\-share\-picker\f[R] and
\f[B]emulate_sway\f[R] are created.
.TP
\f[B]XDG_STATE_HOME\f[R]
Where output layouts are remembered.
Defaults to \f[I]\(ti/.local/state\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]XKB_DEFAULT_RULES\f[R], \f[B]XKB_DEFAULT_MODEL\f[R], \f[B]XKB_DEFAULT_LAYOUT\f[R], \f[B]XKB_DEFAULT_VARIANT\f[R], \f[B]XKB_DEFAULT_OPTIONS\f[R]
The keymap, for whatever \f[B][keyboard]\f[R] leaves out.
.TP
\f[B]XCURSOR_THEME\f[R], \f[B]XCURSOR_SIZE\f[R]
The cursor theme, unless set in the configuration file.
.TP
\f[B]CANOE_OUTPUT_PROFILES\f[R]
File to remember output layouts in, instead of the default.
The headless backend remembers none unless this is set.
.TP
\f[B]CANOE_HEADLESS_OUTPUTS\f[R]
Outputs of the headless backend, as comma\-separated
\f[I]WIDTH\f[R]x\f[I]HEIGHT\f[R][\(at\f[I]SCALE\f[R]],
e.g.\ \f[B]1920x1080\(at1.5,1280x720\f[R].
Default: \f[B]1280x720\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]CANOE_HEADLESS_REVERSE\f[R]
If set, the headless outputs are announced last to first.
.TP
\f[B]CANOE_HEADLESS_LID\f[R] = \f[B]open\f[R] | \f[B]closed\f[R]
Make the first headless output a laptop\(cqs panel with its lid open or
closed.
.SS Set by Canoe
Programs Canoe launches see \f[B]WAYLAND_DISPLAY\f[R] naming its socket,
\f[B]DISPLAY\f[R] naming Xwayland, \f[B]XDG_SESSION_TYPE=wayland\f[R],
\f[B]SWAYSOCK\f[R] if \f[B]emulate_sway\f[R] is on, and the
\f[B][env]\f[R] table.
.SH FILES
.TP
\f[I]\(ti/.config/canoe/canoe.toml\f[R]
The configuration file.
.TP
\f[I]\(ti/.config/canoe/icons/\f[R]
Icons for minimized windows, named \f[I]APP\-ID.svg\f[R] or
\f[I]APP\-ID.png\f[R].
.TP
\f[I]\(ti/.local/state/canoe/outputs.toml\f[R]
Remembered output layouts.
.TP
\f[I]$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/canoe\-WAYLAND_DISPLAY.sock\f[R]
The socket \f[B]canoe \-\-share\-picker\f[R] talks to.
.TP
\f[I]/usr/share/wayland\-sessions/canoe.desktop\f[R]
Session file for display managers, shipped as
\f[I]contrib/canoe.desktop\f[R].
.SH EXIT STATUS
\f[B]canoe\f[R] exits with 0 when it is asked to quit, 1 if it can\(cqt
start (no seat, no GPU, \&...), and 2 for unknown options.
.PP
\f[B]canoe \-\-share\-picker\f[R] exits with 0 when the running Canoe
answered, whether something was picked or not, and 1 if it can\(cqt be
reached.
